  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAll eyes this morning will be on the developing thunderstorm \ncomplexes currently in northern Minnesota and over eastern Lake \nSuperior. Evolution has been slow thus far and IR imagery showing \ncloud top cooling is minimal at this point although growing in size \nover Whitefish Bay, and expect enhancement over the next few hours. \nBulk shear vectors will take these developing complexes \nsoutheastward into the southern Great Lakes, where CAMs/rapid update \nmodels have had a hard time providing any consistency to their \nsolutions on where MCSs will go and if/when they decay. As they move \ntowards the Cleveland CWA, plenty of elevated instability to work \nwith, but atmospheric layer above the boundary layer needs some work \nin terms of RH as it is pretty dry for the time being. Outside of \nthe drier layer of air aloft, surface dewpoints in the mid 60s and \nusable mid level lapse rates exist. However, the timing of the MCS \nworks against it for our area as it tracks southeastward towards \ndawn, and will likely suffer decay as cloud tops are forced to warm \nwith minimal low level jet energy in place. That said, there is an \nexpectation of a few lingering clusters of showers or showers with \nisolated thunder this morning from the Lake Superior MCS. Further \nwest, the Minnesota system is one that hi res models and CAMs are \nsuggesting will survive into the daytime, and could affect our CWA \nlater today. Ultimately, the forecast challenge here is that the \nrest of the day and into the overnight is likely going to rely on \nwhat happens with these initial systems, how long it takes or if the \natmosphere needs to recover from them, if there are lingering wake \nMCVs, etc. Expect an evolving forecast, but know that the chances \nfor convective activity will exist from this afternoon into the \novernight hours. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA cold front will come through Tuesday morning with a wholesale \nairmass change with cooler and drier air in place. Additional upper \ntrough axes will swing through the CWA north to south, and the \nonshore flow coupled with this could generate showers with some lake \nenhancement as the 850mb temperatures drop into the lower teens. \nHigh pressure will then settle into the southern Great Lakes for the \nend of the week with temperatures in the mid 70s to lower 80s \nthrough Thursday.",
